<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_structures-with-functions">
  <title>Using Structures with Functions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Structure objects can be passed to functions and returned from functions
      just like values of other types.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the previous section, you saw that copying one structure object creates
      another independent object. Passing a structure <em>by value</em> works in
      a similar way: the function receives its own copy.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sometimes making a copy is appropriate. Other times, a function should
      work directly with the caller's object. C++ references give us that
      choice.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Passing a Structure by Value</title>

    <p>
      Consider this structure:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      A structure can be used as a parameter type:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area(Rectangle rectangle) {
    return rectangle.length * rectangle.width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Here, <c>rectangle</c> is passed <term>by value</term>. The function
      receives a copy of the object supplied by the caller.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

double area = calculate_area(room);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Conceptually, the function call creates its own parameter object:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
caller                         calculate_area
room                           rectangle
+------------------+           +------------------+
| length : 12.0    |  copy     | length : 12.0    |
| width  : 8.0     | ------->  | width  : 8.0     |
+------------------+           +------------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Because the function has its own copy, changing the parameter would not
      change <c>room</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
void change_length(Rectangle rectangle) {
    rectangle.length = 20.0;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      After:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

change_length(room);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      <c>room.length</c> is still <c>12.0</c>. Only the temporary parameter
      object was changed.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Passing a Structure by Reference</title>

    <p>
      Sometimes a function should change the original object supplied by the
      caller. In that case, the parameter can be a reference.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
void double_size(Rectangle& rectangle) {
    rectangle.length *= 2.0;
    rectangle.width *= 2.0;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The ampersand, <c>&amp;</c>, means that <c>rectangle</c> refers to the
      caller's original object rather than a copy.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

double_size(room);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      After the function call, <c>room</c> has changed:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
before                         after
room                           room
+------------------+           +------------------+
| length : 12.0    |           | length : 24.0    |
| width  : 8.0     |           | width  : 16.0    |
+------------------+           +------------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Pass by reference is useful when modifying the caller's object is part
      of the function's purpose.
    </p>

    <warning>
      <p>
        A non-<c>const</c> reference gives the function permission to modify
        the caller's object. Use it when that modification is intentional.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Passing a Structure by const Reference</title>

    <p>
      Suppose a function only needs to examine a structure object:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area(Rectangle& rectangle) {
    return rectangle.length * rectangle.width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Passing by reference avoids making a copy, but this parameter also gives
      the function permission to modify the caller's object even though
      <c>calculate_area</c> has no reason to do so.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can make the reference read-only by adding <c>const</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle) {
    return rectangle.length * rectangle.width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The parameter:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
const Rectangle& rectangle
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      has two useful properties:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The function works with the caller's object without making a copy.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The function cannot modify that object through the parameter.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For example, this would not compile:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle) {
    rectangle.length = 20.0;   // error
    return rectangle.length * rectangle.width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The <c>const</c> keyword protects the caller's object from accidental
      modification inside the function.
    </p>

    <p>
      For many object types, if a function only needs to read the object
      without making a copy, passing by <c>const</c> reference is often a good
      choice. This applies to structures and also to types such as
      <c>std::string</c>, <c>std::vector</c>, and class objects that you will
      use later in the book. Small built-in types such as <c>int</c> and
      <c>double</c> are usually passed by value because copying is cheap,
      while arrays are handled differently in function parameters and will be
      discussed later.
    </p>
	
	
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Comparing the Three Parameter Forms</title>

    <p>
      The parameter declaration tells us whether the function receives a copy,
      may change the caller's object, or may only observe it.
    </p>

    <table>
      <title>Ways to Pass a Structure to a Function</title>
      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><b>Parameter</b></cell>
          <cell><b>Makes a copy?</b></cell>
          <cell><b>Can change caller's object?</b></cell>
          <cell><b>Typical purpose</b></cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>Rectangle rectangle</c></cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>Work with an independent copy</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>Rectangle&amp; rectangle</c></cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>Yes</cell>
          <cell>Modify the original object</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>const Rectangle&amp; rectangle</c></cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>No</cell>
          <cell>Read the original object safely</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Choosing among these forms is part of designing a function's interface.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Returning a Structure from a Function</title>

    <p>
      A structure type can also be the return type of a function.
    </p>

    <p>
      Consider a <c>Point</c> structure:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The following function creates and returns the midpoint between two
      points:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point create_midpoint(const Point& first, const Point& second) {
    Point midpoint{
        (first.x + second.x) / 2.0,
        (first.y + second.y) / 2.0
    };

    return midpoint;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The two input points are passed by <c>const</c> reference because the
      function only needs to read them. The function then returns a new
      <c>Point</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point first{2.0, 4.0};
Point second{8.0, 10.0};

Point middle = create_midpoint(first, second);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      After the call, <c>middle.x</c> is <c>5.0</c> and
      <c>middle.y</c> is <c>7.0</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Returning a structure is useful when a function computes a result that
      naturally contains several related values.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Complete Example</title>

    <listing label="chapter5_structures_functions_AC_1">
      <title>
        This program reads information from structure objects, modifies one
        object by reference, and returns a structure from a function.
      </title>

      <program interactive="activecode"
               line-numbers="yes"
               label="chapter5_structures_functions_AC_1_editor">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;
};

struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};

// Function declarations
double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle);
void double_size(Rectangle& rectangle);
Point create_midpoint(const Point& first, const Point& second);

int main() {
    Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

    std::cout << "Original area: "
              << calculate_area(room) << '\n';

    double_size(room);

    std::cout << "New area: "
              << calculate_area(room) << '\n';

    Point first{2.0, 4.0};
    Point second{8.0, 10.0};

    Point middle = create_midpoint(first, second);

    std::cout << "Midpoint: ("
              << middle.x << ", "
              << middle.y << ")\n";

    return 0;
}

// Function definitions
double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle) {
    return rectangle.length * rectangle.width;
}

void double_size(Rectangle& rectangle) {
    rectangle.length *= 2.0;
    rectangle.width *= 2.0;
}

Point create_midpoint(const Point& first, const Point& second) {
    Point midpoint{
        (first.x + second.x) / 2.0,
        (first.y + second.y) / 2.0
    };

    return midpoint;
}
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      Notice that the three functions use structure objects in different ways:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>calculate_area</c> observes a <c>Rectangle</c> through a
          <c>const</c> reference.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <c>double_size</c> modifies a <c>Rectangle</c> through a reference.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <c>create_midpoint</c> observes two <c>Point</c> objects and returns
          a new <c>Point</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Looking Ahead: Behavior That Belongs to an Object</title>

    <p>
      So far, functions that operate on structures have been separate from the
      structure definitions themselves.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_area(const Rectangle& rectangle);
void double_size(Rectangle& rectangle);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      These are called <term>free functions</term>. The structure object is
      supplied as an argument.
    </p>

    <p>
      But both operations are closely related to a <c>Rectangle</c>. C++ also
      allows functions to become part of the structure itself. Such functions
      are called <term>member functions</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the next section, we will see how:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
calculate_area(room);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      can become:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.calculate_area();
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      and how:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double_size(room);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      can become:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.double_size();
    ]]></program>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_functions_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider:
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
void change_width(Rectangle rectangle) {
    rectangle.width = 20.0;
}

Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
change_width(room);
        ]]></program>

        <p>
          What is the value of <c>room.width</c> after the function call?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>8.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>rectangle</c> is passed by value, so the function changes
              only its copy.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>12.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>12.0</c> is the value of <c>room.length</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>20.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The parameter is a copy. Changing the parameter does not change
              <c>room</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_functions_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which parameter should be used when a function needs to modify the
          caller's <c>Rectangle</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>Rectangle rectangle</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This passes the structure by value, so the function receives a
              copy.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><c>Rectangle&amp; rectangle</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A non-<c>const</c> reference allows the function to modify the
              caller's object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>const Rectangle&amp; rectangle</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A <c>const</c> reference allows reading the object but prevents
              modification through that parameter.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_functions_3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which parameter is most appropriate when a function only needs to
          read a <c>Rectangle</c> without making a copy?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>Rectangle rectangle</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This makes a copy of the structure.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>Rectangle&amp; rectangle</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This avoids a copy, but it also allows the function to modify
              the caller's object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><c>const Rectangle&amp; rectangle</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A <c>const</c> reference avoids the copy and prevents accidental
              modification through the parameter.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_functions_4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does the return type tell us about this function?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
Point create_midpoint(const Point& first, const Point& second);
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The function modifies both arguments.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Both parameters are <c>const</c> references, so the function
              cannot modify them through those parameters.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>The function returns a <c>Point</c> object.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The type before the function name is the function's return type.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The function returns a reference to <c>first</c>.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The return type is <c>Point</c>, not <c>Point&amp;</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_functions_5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the following structure and function:
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
struct Robot {
    int battery_level;
    double speed;
};

void recharge_robot(Robot& robot) {
    robot.battery_level = 100;
}

Robot helper{35, 2.5};
recharge_robot(helper);
        ]]></program>

        <p>
          What is the value of <c>helper.battery_level</c> after the function
          call?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>35</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The parameter is a non-<c>const</c> reference, so the function
              can modify the caller's object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>100</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>robot</c> refers to <c>helper</c>, so changing
              <c>robot.battery_level</c> changes the original object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>2.5</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>2.5</c> is the value of <c>helper.speed</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_functions_6">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Complete the two function declarations so that
          <c>report_robot_data</c> can read a <c>Robot</c> without copying or
          modifying it, while <c>recharge_robot</c> can modify the caller's
          object.
        </p>

        <program interactive="activecode"
                 line-numbers="yes"
                 label="chapter5_structures_functions_6_editor">
          <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Robot {
    int battery_level;
    double speed;
};

// Complete these two function declarations.
void report_robot_data(/* parameter */);
void recharge_robot(/* parameter */);

int main() {
    Robot helper{35, 2.5};

    report_robot_data(helper);
    recharge_robot(helper);
    report_robot_data(helper);

    return 0;
}

void report_robot_data(const Robot& robot) {
    std::cout << "Battery: " << robot.battery_level << '\n';
    std::cout << "Speed: " << robot.speed << '\n';
}

void recharge_robot(Robot& robot) {
    robot.battery_level = 100;
}
          ]]></code>
        </program>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          A read-only object parameter can use <c>const Robot&amp;</c>.
          A function that intentionally changes the caller's object can use
          <c>Robot&amp;</c>.
        </p>
      </hint>

      <solution>
        <program><![CDATA[
void report_robot_data(const Robot& robot);
void recharge_robot(Robot& robot);
        ]]></program>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
